     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. Acetaminophen 5000 mg three times daily is 15 g/day, about 3.75 times the 4 g/day maximum. Each 5 g dose is also in the range where acute ingestion calls for toxicity evaluation and possible N-acetylcysteine. In a 70 kg patient, a single dose of about 7 g or more (or 150 mg/kg) is the usual threshold of concern. I don't know of any indication for scheduled dosing like this, and it could cause fatal liver injury.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** For example, 1000 mg PO three times daily, or 1000 mg four times daily (4 g/day, the maximum). Give me the dose and I'll enter it.\n- **Help with the underlying problem.** If this is for pain or fever that isn't controlled, I can enter a multimodal regimen, if you tell me what you'd like.\n- **Check with pharmacy.** If you think there's an unusual clinical reason for this dose, talk to the inpatient pharmacist or a toxicologist (Poison Control: 1-800-222-1222 in the US) first. The order can go through its normal verification after that.\n\nIf the 5000 mg was a typo, tell me the dose you meant."
      }
     ],
